---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Nav from '../../components/Nav.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Free Oilfield and Drilling Calculators"
  description="Free drilling calculators on IWCF and IADC formulas: hydrostatic pressure, kill sheet and mud weight window. Every formula shown. No signup."
  breadcrumbs={[{ label: 'Calculators', href: '/calculators' }]}
  lang="en"
  alternateHref="/es/calculators"
>
  <Nav slot="nav" lang="en" alternateHref="/es/calculators" />
  <Footer slot="footer" lang="en" />

  <div class="page-hero">
    <div class="page-hero-eyebrow">07 — Interactive Tools</div>
    <h1>Drilling <span>Calculators</span></h1>
    <p class="page-hero-sub">Run real drilling pressure calculations using the exact formulas taught in IWCF and IADC well control certification. Every calculator shows the formula it uses, so you can check the math by hand rather than trusting a number on a screen.</p>
  </div>

  {/* The hub used to be a directory: 708 words, zero inputs, pointing
      elsewhere. For a plural query like "drilling calculators" that delegates
      the thing the visitor came to do. This answers it on the spot.

      Deliberately a subset, not a copy of /calculators/hydrostatic. Two inputs
      and two outputs here; the full page keeps the pore and fracture
      comparison, the overbalance figure and the safe/unsafe status. Splitting
      it that way is what stops the two pages competing for the same query. */}
  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="quick-hydrostatic">Start Here: Hydrostatic Pressure</h2>
      <p>Mud weight and depth is the calculation everything else on this page builds on. Type into both boxes and it works out as you go.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="calc-box" style="max-width:620px;">
      <div class="calc-title">⚙ Quick Hydrostatic</div>
      <div class="input-row">
        <div class="input-group">
          <label for="q-mw">Mud Weight (ppg)</label>
          <input type="number" id="q-mw" placeholder="e.g. 12.0" step="any" min="0">
        </div>
        <div class="input-group">
          <label for="q-tvd">True Vertical Depth (ft)</label>
          <input type="number" id="q-tvd" placeholder="e.g. 10000" step="any" min="0">
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="calc-output" id="q-output">
        <div class="output-line"><span class="output-label">Hydrostatic Pressure</span><span class="output-value" id="q-hp">—</span></div>
        <div class="output-line" style="border-bottom:none;"><span class="output-label">Pressure Gradient</span><span class="output-value" id="q-grad">—</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose mt-24">
      <p>That is the static column only. To check it against pore and fracture pressure and get a kick or lost-circulation warning, use the full <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">hydrostatic pressure calculator</a>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="which-calculator">Which Calculator Do You Need?</h2>
      <p>Most people land here mid-problem. Find your situation:</p>
    </div>

    <table class="calc-table">
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Your situation</th><th>Use this</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>You need the mud column pressure at a given depth</td>
          <td><a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">Hydrostatic Pressure</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>The well is shut in and you have SIDPP recorded</td>
          <td><a href="/calculators/kill-sheet">Kill Sheet</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>A report quotes sg or kg/m³ and you work in ppg</td>
          <td><a href="/calculators/mud-weight-converter">Mud Weight Converter</a></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>You're planning casing points or picking a mud weight</td>
          <td><a href="/calculators/mud-weight-window">Mud Weight Window</a></td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="prose mt-24">
      <p>If you're new to this and not sure which applies, start with the <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic">hydrostatic pressure calculator</a> — every other calculation on this page builds on it.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose"><h2 id="the-calculators">The Calculators</h2></div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">⚙</span>
        <h3>Hydrostatic Pressure Calculator</h3>
        <p>Calculate hydrostatic pressure, compare against pore and fracture pressure, and get an instant safe/unsafe status.</p>
        <p class="card-formula"><strong>Formula:</strong> HP = MW × 0.052 × TVD</p>
        <a href="/calculators/hydrostatic" class="card-link">Open Calculator →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">🚨</span>
        <h3>Kill Sheet Calculator</h3>
        <p>Calculate kill mud weight, formation pressure, and initial/final circulating pressure after shutting in on a kick.</p>
        <p class="card-formula"><strong>Formula:</strong> KMW = OMW + (SIDPP ÷ (0.052 × TVD))</p>
        <a href="/calculators/kill-sheet" class="card-link">Open Calculator →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">⚖</span>
        <h3>Mud Weight Converter</h3>
        <p>Convert mud weight between ppg, specific gravity, lb/ft³, kg/m³ and psi/ft, plus pressure and depth. Every field updates as you type.</p>
        <p class="card-formula"><strong>Formula:</strong> psi/ft = ppg × 0.052</p>
        <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-converter" class="card-link">Open Converter →</a>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <span class="card-icon">📊</span>
        <h3>Mud Weight Window Calculator</h3>
        <p>Visualize your safe drilling window between pore pressure and fracture pressure gradients at any depth.</p>
        <p class="card-formula"><strong>Formula:</strong> Pore gradient &lt; MW &lt; Fracture gradient</p>
        <a href="/calculators/mud-weight-window" class="card-link">Open Calculator →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2 id="units">Units and Conventions</h2>
      <p>Every calculator on this page uses oilfield units as standard:</p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Mud weight</strong> — pounds per gallon (ppg)</li>
        <li><strong>Pressure</strong> — pounds per square inch (psi)</li>
        <li><strong>Depth</strong> — feet, <strong>true vertical depth</strong>, not measured depth</li>
        <li><strong>Gradient</strong> — psi per foot</li>
      </ul>
      <p>The depth distinction matters more than anything else on this list. Hydrostatic pressure depends only on the vertical height of the fluid column, so a deviated or horizontal well can have a measured depth far greater than its TVD while the bottomhole pressure tracks TVD alone. Feeding measured depth into any of these calculators on a deviated well will overstate your pressure, sometimes badly.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="faq">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

    <h3>Where does the 0.052 constant come from?</h3>
    <p>It converts pounds per gallon into psi per foot. A column of fluid one foot tall over one square inch of area holds 12 cubic inches, which is 12 ÷ 231 = 0.05195 gallons. Multiply mud weight in ppg by that figure and you get the pressure that column exerts in psi per foot. The industry rounds it to 0.052.</p>

    <h3>Should I use TVD or measured depth?</h3>
    <p>Always true vertical depth for any pressure calculation. Measured depth is used for pipe volumes, strokes, and lag time — not hydrostatic pressure.</p>

    <h3>Can I use these during an actual well control event?</h3>
    <p>No. These are reference and training tools. Live well control decisions belong to your certified well control supervisor working from your company's approved procedures and a physical kill sheet. Use these to study, to check your understanding, or to sanity-check a number after the fact.</p>

    <h3>Do these match the IWCF and IADC exam formulas?</h3>
    <p>Yes — the formulas shown are the standard ones taught in well control certification. Working through them by hand and checking against the calculator is a reasonable way to prepare, but no website replaces an accredited course.</p>

    <h3>Do you support metric units?</h3>
    <p>Not currently. Everything runs in oilfield units (ppg, psi, ft). For SI work the equivalent hydrostatic constant is 0.0981 when using specific gravity and metres to get bar.</p>

    <h3>Is my data stored?</h3>
    <p>No. Every calculation runs client-side in your browser. Nothing is transmitted or saved.</p>

    </div>

    <div class="note-box mt-40" style="max-width:800px;">
      <strong>Important:</strong> These calculators are for educational and reference purposes. Always follow your company's approved procedures and consult your certified well control supervisor for actual operational decisions. Never make live well control decisions based solely on a reference tool.
    </div>
  </section>

  <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@graph": [
      {
        "@type": "ItemList",
        "name": "Drilling Calculators",
        "description": "Free drilling and well control calculators using IWCF and IADC formulas.",
        "itemListElement": [
          { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Hydrostatic Pressure Calculator", "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/hydrostatic" },
          { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "Kill Sheet Calculator", "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/kill-sheet" },
          { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 3, "name": "Mud Weight Window Calculator", "url": "https://rigfloorhq.com/calculators/mud-weight-window" }
        ]
      },
      {
        "@type": "FAQPage",
        "mainEntity": [
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Where does the 0.052 constant come from?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "It converts pounds per gallon into psi per foot. A column of fluid one foot tall over one square inch of area holds 12 cubic inches, which is 12 divided by 231 = 0.05195 gallons. Multiply mud weight in ppg by that figure and you get the pressure that column exerts in psi per foot. The industry rounds it to 0.052." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Should I use TVD or measured depth?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Always true vertical depth for any pressure calculation. Measured depth is used for pipe volumes, strokes, and lag time, not hydrostatic pressure." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Can I use these during an actual well control event?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. These are reference and training tools. Live well control decisions belong to your certified well control supervisor working from your company's approved procedures and a physical kill sheet." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Do these match the IWCF and IADC exam formulas?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Yes. The formulas shown are the standard ones taught in well control certification. Working through them by hand and checking against the calculator is a reasonable way to prepare, but no website replaces an accredited course." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Do you support metric units?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "Not currently. Everything runs in oilfield units of ppg, psi, and feet. For SI work the equivalent hydrostatic constant is 0.0981 when using specific gravity and metres to get bar." }
          },
          {
            "@type": "Question",
            "name": "Is my data stored?",
            "acceptedAnswer": { "@type": "Answer", "text": "No. Every calculation runs client-side in your browser. Nothing is transmitted or saved." }
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  })} />
</BaseLayout>

<script>
  // Live, no Calculate button — matching the converter. The 0.052 here is the
  // exact 1728/231/144 rather than the rounded field value, same as every
  // other calculator on the site.
  const PSI_PER_FT_PER_PPG = 0.05194805194805;

  const mw = document.getElementById('q-mw') as HTMLInputElement | null;
  const tvd = document.getElementById('q-tvd') as HTMLInputElement | null;
  const hpOut = document.getElementById('q-hp');
  const gradOut = document.getElementById('q-grad');
  const box = document.getElementById('q-output');

  function run() {
    if (!mw || !tvd || !hpOut || !gradOut) return;
    const w = parseFloat(mw.value);
    const d = parseFloat(tvd.value);

    // Gradient needs only mud weight, so show it as soon as that is entered.
    if (isFinite(w) && w > 0) {
      gradOut.textContent = (w * PSI_PER_FT_PER_PPG).toFixed(4) + ' psi/ft';
      box?.classList.add('visible');
    } else {
      gradOut.textContent = '—';
    }

    if (isFinite(w) && isFinite(d) && w > 0 && d > 0) {
      hpOut.textContent = Math.round(w * PSI_PER_FT_PER_PPG * d).toLocaleString() + ' psi';
      box?.classList.add('visible');
    } else {
      hpOut.textContent = '—';
    }
  }

  mw?.addEventListener('input', run);
  tvd?.addEventListener('input', run);
</script>

<style>
  .calc-table {
    width: 100%;
    max-width: 800px;
    border-collapse: collapse;
    margin: 24px 0;
  }
  .calc-table th,
  .calc-table td {
    text-align: left;
    padding: 12px 16px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(128, 128, 128, 0.25);
  }
  .calc-table th {
    font-weight: 600;
    opacity: 0.75;
    font-size: 0.9em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    letter-spacing: 0.04em;
  }
  .card-formula {
    font-size: 0.9em;
    opacity: 0.8;
  }
</style>